{"name":"Tower Bridge","hex":"#7ba0a0","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Tower Bridge","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A bascule bridge in London crossing the River Thames, built in the late 19th century"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Tower_Bridge"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tower Bridge","blocks":[{"text":"Tower Bridge is a Grade I listed combined bascule, suspension, and, until 1960, cantilever bridge in London, built between 1886 and 1894, designed by Horace Jones and engineered by John Wolfe Barry with the help of Henry Marc Brunel. It crosses the River Thames close to the Tower of London and is one of five London bridges owned and maintained by the City Bridge Foundation, a charitable trust founded in 1282."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Tower_Bridge_at_Dawn.jpg/330px-Tower_Bridge_at_Dawn.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tower_Bridge"},{"title":"Tower Bridge (Sacramento, California)","blocks":[{"text":"The Tower Bridge is a vertical lift bridge across the Sacramento River, linking West Sacramento in Yolo County to the west, with the capital of California, Sacramento, in Sacramento County to the east. It has also been known as M Street Bridge. It was previously a part of U.S. Route 40 until that highway was truncated to east of Salt Lake City as well as US Route 99W, which served the western portion of the Sacramento Valley from Sacramento to Red Bluff. The bridge is maintained by the California Department of Transportation as State Route 275 and connects West Capitol Avenue and Cabaldon Parkway in West Sacramento with the Capitol Mall in Sacramento."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Tower_Bridge_Sacramento_edit.jpg/330px-Tower_Bridge_Sacramento_edit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tower_Bridge_(Sacramento%2C_California)"},{"title":"Tower","blocks":[{"text":"A tower is a tall structure, taller than it is wide, often by a significant factor. Towers are distinguished from masts by their lack of guy-wires and are therefore, along with tall buildings, self-supporting structures."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Tokyo_Sky_Tree_2012.JPG/330px-Tokyo_Sky_Tree_2012.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tower"},{"title":"Bridge","blocks":[{"text":"A bridge is a structure designed to span an obstacle, such as a river or railway, allowing vehicles, pedestrians, and other loads to pass across. Most bridges consist of a flat deck, supported by beams, arches, or cables. These structures rest on a foundation that is carefully designed to transfer the weight of the bridge to the subsoil without settling."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Bridge_Alcantara.JPG/330px-Bridge_Alcantara.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bridge"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Tower_bridge_schm020.png/500px-Tower_bridge_schm020.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Tower_bridge_schm020.png/500px-Tower_bridge_schm020.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Elevation, with dimensions","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATower_bridge_schm020.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Tower_bridge_works_1892.jpg/500px-Tower_bridge_works_1892.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Tower_bridge_works_1892.jpg/500px-Tower_bridge_works_1892.jpg